Round complete. Both deliverable files are written (`impl/gen_rader.c`, `strategies/gen_rader.md`), the shipped source is exactly what the final tryout measured, and all three gates pass with the chain bit-identical across processes.

**Summary of what happened this round:** I replaced the stub with a genuine folded-Rader engine: the conjugate fold plus multiplicative reindexing turns the 31-point DFT into one cyclic-15 and one negacyclic-15 convolution (the negacyclic converts to cyclic via free sign twists since 15 is odd), each computed as Winograd-C3 nested over dense cyclic-5 blocks — 100 FMA + 65 adds per convolution, all-real constants, ~430 zmm ops per 4 pencils vs the dense rival's ~555. The chassis (pass order, chain scheme, s6 map) is adopted from gen_dense_prime with credit. The two other big wins were routing the z-pass through the same kernel via 4×4-complex tile transposes (−8 us) and making every pass in-place so the chain's working set fits L2 at 953 KB (−16 us). Map-fusion into the y-stores and a single-masked-body kernel were both built, measured slower, and recorded as negatives. A create()-time self-check against a dense reference guards against ever shipping a wrong fast path. Result: **94 us/step vs gen_dense_prime's 177.9 on the graded cell** — the crossover fight at L=31 currently goes to Rader by 1.9x.

Technique: folded Rader-31 → cyclic-15 + twisted-negacyclic-15 via Winograd-C3 × dense-C5, fully in-place L2-resident fused chain. Best on the node: **94.0 us/transform at B=16 (m=140 graded chain), 93.1 us at B=1**; rel L2 = 4.06e-16 single, 2.55e-14 chain m=140, 1.70e-15 two-step.
